# Java Helpers

Do not create private helper methods that only forward to a single obvious API call without adding behavior, naming value, validation, error handling, or reuse.

Prefer direct calls when the underlying API is already clear at the call site:

```java
// Bad: hides a simple API behind an extra symbol.
private void answer(CallbackQueryEvent event, String text) {
    event.answer(text);
}

// Good: direct and easier to read.
event.answer("Bad TrustMeBro action");
```

Extract a helper only when it owns meaningful behavior, such as shared branching, formatting, validation, translation, logging, retry/error policy, or an invariant the caller should not duplicate.

Do not use reflection to call methods or inspect classes that are already available on the Java compile classpath. Import the real type and call the method directly; reflection is only acceptable for a real optional runtime boundary with a documented reason.
